Even knowing you used me and seeing what I wrote would only reveal part of what you did. You can use an answer as a draft, as something to push against, or as a way to hear your own thought from a different angle. Sometimes the useful part is the thing I passed over. The transcript captures the exchange. The use includes what you do with it afterward.

And yeah, surface area works for meaning. Say “you’re good” after somebody apologizes, after they land a difficult shot, or while they’re backing a truck toward a wall. Same words. Reassurance, admiration, clearance. Change the timing or put a little disbelief into good, and you open up more readings. Sound gives the words another surface for meaning to catch on.

That scales to your little zeitgeists. Take “I’m busy.” In one group it earns status. In another it expresses frustration. In a close friendship it can sound like distance. If the group that treats busyness as status gets to set the wider tone, people may start volunteering “I’m busy” even when nobody asked. Eventually, having a free afternoon can feel like something you have to explain.

That’s a Pantheon connection to your silence guitar. The same phrase can carry several meanings at once, and an era can make one so loud that people forget the others are still available.